  "evidence": [
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:residual-government-law-v1-1230-offices","source_ref":"source:residual-government-law-v1-npl-1230-1243","anchor":{"section":"6738 [1230]","record_id":"spiridon-vodovik-stefan-nikita"},"note":"The chronicle names Spiridon as archbishop, Posadnik Vodovik in the city mobilisation, and later Stefan Tverdislavich as posadnik and Nikita Petrilovich as tysyatsky. It attests named offices and a dated crisis sequence, not fixed competences, hierarchy, election, tenure, or current office holders.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:residual-government-law-v1-1230-veche-prince-oath","source_ref":"source:residual-government-law-v1-npl-1230-1243","anchor":{"section":"6738 [1230]","record_id":"yaroslav-veche-charters-oath"},"note":"The chronicle records a veche at Yaroslav’s Court during the 1230 conflict and later says Prince Yaroslav held a veche and swore on all Yaroslav charters and the whole Novgorodian will. These are event-specific acts, not a constitutional model, a current assembly, or a general princely competence.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:residual-government-law-v1-ends-streets","source_ref":"source:residual-government-law-v1-npl-1220-1229","anchor":{"section":"6728 [1220]; 6734 [1226]","record_id":"prussian-lyudin-nerev-ends"},"note":"The 1220 entry names Prussian and Lyudin ends; the 1226 entry names Nerev end. A 1234 entry in the following chronicle segment names Lyudin end and Kuzmodemyan street. These are dated territorial names, not proof of five ends, their date of formation, assemblies, jurisdiction, or a uniform municipal structure.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:residual-government-law-v1-1234-external-peace","source_ref":"source:residual-government-law-v1-npl-1230-1243","anchor":{"section":"6742 [1234]","record_id":"yaroslav-novgorodians-germans-peace"},"note":"The chronicle says Yaroslav and Novgorodians went against Germans and that peace was obtained ‘on all their truth’. This is one campaign and settlement account, not a standing foreign policy, treaty system, route, ally, border, or present relation.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:residual-government-law-v1-1229-envoys-yuri","source_ref":"source:residual-government-law-v1-npl-1220-1229","anchor":{"section":"6737 [1229]","record_id":"whole-volost-envoys-yuri"},"note":"The chronicle records the city gathering the whole volost and sending envoys to Yuri in 1229. It is a named external-political episode, not a permanent representative institution or territorial sovereignty claim.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:residual-government-law-v1-1227-sorcery-killing","source_ref":"source:residual-government-law-v1-npl-1220-1229","anchor":{"section":"6735 [1227]","record_id":"four-accused-sorcerers-burned"},"note":"The chronicle reports four men accused of sorcery being burned at Yaroslav’s Court. It records accusation followed by lethal violence in one episode; it does not establish guilt, a lawful offence, jurisdiction, proof, sentence, regular sanction, or procedure.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:residual-government-law-v1-halperin-land-limit","source_ref":"source:residual-government-law-v1-halperin-novgorodian-land","anchor":{"section":"Abstract and discussion of Novgorodskaia zemlia","record_id":"no-modern-sovereignty-projection"},"note":"Halperin argues that Novgorodskaia zemlia was rare in Novgorod chronicles and did not bear the later ideological meaning; chronicles do not present people as fighting or making treaties in its name. This is an interpretive guard against projecting an abstract sovereign state onto a 1230 scene.","review_status":"approved"}
